\use{document}

\title {Your Paper}
\author {You}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Your abstract.
\end{abstract}

\section {Introduction}

Your introduction goes here! Simply start writing your document and use the
preview to see the result. Examples of commonly used commands and features are
listed below, to help you get started.

Notice that the first paragraph after a heading is set flush to the left margin,
with no first-line indent, exactly as in a LaTeX \emph{article}. The paragraphs
that follow it, like this one, open with the usual first-line indent and there is
no extra vertical space between them — the indent alone marks the boundary.

\section {Some examples to get started}

\subsection {How to create Sections and Subsections}

Simply use the section and subsection commands, as in this example document! All
the formatting and numbering is handled automatically: a subsection's number is
reset to one at the start of each new section.

\subsection {How to add Lists}

You can make lists with automatic numbering \dots

\begin{enumerate}
\item Like this,
\item and like this.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\dots or bullet points \dots

\begin{itemize}
\item Like this,

\item and like this, with an item long enough to wrap onto a second line so that
the continuation lines align neatly under the text rather than under the marker,

\item including a nested list inside an item:

\begin{itemize}
\item a nested bullet,

\item and another nested bullet, also long enough to wrap, so the deeper hanging
indent stands out against the outer level.
\end{itemize}

\item and back out at the first level for the final item.
\end{itemize}

\subsection {How to add Quotations}

A quotation is indented on both sides and remains fully justified:

\begin{quote}
The art of writing is the art of discovering what you believe. Good prose is like
a windowpane, clear enough that the reader forgets it is there at all and sees
only what lies beyond it.
\end{quote}

The text returns to the full measure after the block closes.

\subsection {How to change the margins and paper size}

Usually the format you are using will have the page margins and paper size set
correctly for that use-case. In these cases, it is best not to alter the margins
directly. This format follows the proportions of a 10-point \LaTeX article: a
serif body on a tight type scale, with roman titles and \textbf{bold} headings.
The same engine that sets the \TeX, \LaTeX and \TeXish logos sets this document.

\vfill\eject
